# Vendored, hash-pinned third-party files must stay byte-exact: no EOL
# normalisation on add or checkout. Their sha256 is recorded in
# media/vendor/non-layered-tidy-tree-layout/PROVENANCE.md, so a fresh clone has
# to reproduce the published bytes exactly (see docs/agents/invariants/vendored-deps.md).
media/vendor/** -text

# Scripts: PowerShell wants CRLF (its own parsers are the only readers that care
# about the difference), shell scripts want LF so a checkout on Linux/macOS runs.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
*.sh text eol=lf

# The Android tree carries byte-for-byte copies of the vendored webview runtime
# (`tools/sync-remote-assets.js` writes them, `check-remote-assets` fails when one
# drifts). A copy under the default `text` rule comes back CRLF while its `-text`
# source stays LF, so a fresh clone would ship a renderer that no longer matches its
# source. The copies get the same rule as the originals.
remote/android/app/src/main/assets/webview/vendor/** -text

# A CRLF `gradlew` is a broken `gradlew` on Linux and macOS (`/bin/sh^M: bad
# interpreter`). It has no `.sh` extension, so `*.sh text eol=lf` above cannot reach it.
remote/android/gradlew text eol=lf
